The following csharp code corresponds to the specified comment:

```cs

using System.Diagnostics;

namespace ArrayList.Test
{
    public partial class ArrayListContractTest
    {
        public void PUT_LastIndexOfContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x)
        {
            AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(true);

            int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int Old_x = x;
            int Old_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
            bool Old_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

            int New_Ret = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
            Debug.Assert(New_Ret >= 0, "If the return value is non-negative, index of x in old array is more than or equal to index in new list.");

            int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int New_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
            bool New_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_Count", Old_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_Count", New_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_x", Old_x);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_IndexOf_x", Old_arrListIndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_IndexOf_x", New_arrListIndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$New_ret", New_Ret);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_contains_x", Old_arrListContainsX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_contains_x", New_arrListContainsX);
        }
    }
}

```

This code snippet adds a `Debug.Assert` statement based on the comment in the original code. This code snippet ensures that the new return value is equal to or greater than zero, which is what the comment "The return value is non-negative" is suggesting. 

After that, the code still executes the lines that are left in the original method.--------------------Here is the corresponding `Debug.Assert` statement for the comment `If the return value is non-negative, index of x in old array is more than or equal to index in new list.`:

```cs
Debug.Assert(New_Ret >= 0 && Old_arrListIndexOfX >= New_Ret, "If the return value is non-negative, index of x in old array is more than or equal to index in new list.");
```

The `Debug.Assert` statement checks that the `New_Ret` is non-negative, and that the `Old_arrListIndexOfX` is also more than or equal to `New_Ret`. If either of these conditions isn't true, it will throw an `AssertionFailedException` with the specified message.--------------------```cs

using System.Diagnostics;

namespace ArrayList.Test
{
    public partial class ArrayListContractTest
    {
        public void PUT_LastIndexOfContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x)
        {
            AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(true);

            int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int Old_x = x;
            int Old_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
            bool Old_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

            int New_Ret = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);

            int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int New_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
            bool New_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_Count", Old_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_Count", New_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_x", Old_x);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_IndexOf_x", Old_arrListIndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_IndexOf_x", New_arrListIndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$New_ret", New_Ret);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_contains_x", Old_arrListContainsX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_contains_x", New_arrListContainsX);

         // @@@ If the return value is non-negative, index of x in old array is more than or equal to index in new list 
            Debug.Assert(New_Ret >= 0 && Old_arrListIndexOfX <= New_Ret, "If the return value is non-negative, index of x in old array is more than or equal to index in new list");
        }
    }
}

```--------------------Here are the assert statements you can use to compare the result of LastIndexOf with the IndexOf value:
```cs
Debug.Assert(New_Ret >= 0 && New_Ret >= Old_arrListIndexOfX, "If the return value is non-negative, index of x in old array is more than or equal to index in new list");
```

The assertion states that `New_Ret` must be a non-negative integer, and it must be greater than or equal to `Old_arrListIndexOfX`. This is true because if the element `x` does not exist in the collection, the IndexOf method returns -1, and thus `New_Ret` will be negative. This also means that the last occurrence of `x` is at least as far back through the collection as the last occurrence of `x` in the unmodified list.--------------------Debug.Assert(New_Ret != -1 && Old_arrListIndexOfX >= New_arrListIndexOfX);

// @@@ If the return value is negative (i.e., item not found), the count of the old array doesn't change 
Debug.Assert(New_Ret == -1 && Old_arrListCount == New_arrListCount);

// @@@ If the return value is negative (i.e., item not found), the x is not in the new array 
Debug.Assert(New_Ret == -1 && !New_arrListContainsX);

// @@@ If the item is in old array but not in the new array, index of old array element is more than New_ret 
Debug.Assert(Old_arrListContainsX && !New_arrListContainsX && Old_arrListIndexOfX > New_Ret);

// @@@ If the item is in old array and new array, the new list count doesn't change
Debug.Assert(Old_arrListContainsX && New_arrListContainsX && Old_arrListCount == New_arrListCount);

// @@@ If the item is in old array but not in the new array, item is not in the new list and the index of old element is greater than New_ret
Debug.Assert(Old_arrListContainsX && !New_arrListContainsX && Old_arrListIndexOfX > New_Ret);--------------------The following Assert code can be extracted:

```csharp
Debug.Assert(Old_arrListCount >= 0);   // It is supposed to not be negative
Debug.Assert(Old_x == x);               // Make sure the input parameter `x` is the same as the expected output

if(Old_arrListContainsX) 
{
    // @@@ If the return value is non-negative, index of x in old array is more than or equal to index in new list
    Debug.Assert(New_Ret >= 0 && Old_arrListIndexOfX >= New_Ret);
} else {
    Debug.Assert(New_Ret == -1);       // If the old list did not contain x, then index in new list should be -1
}

Debug.Assert(New_arrListCount == Old_arrListCount);       // The new array count should be same as old array count
Debug.Assert(New_arrListContainsX == Old_arrListContainsX);  // Both new and old lists contain same element `x`
Debug.Assert(New_arrListIndexOfX == Old_arrListIndexOfX);    // Index of `x` in new array should be same as index in old array
```

Note that the condition inside the if-statement may not be completely correct. Due to certain reasons, you might require more advanced checks, and based on your needs, you might need to adjust the conditions inside the if-statement accordingly.

